  "query_preview": "..\n(Friday through next Wednesday)\nIssued at 1225 PM CST Wed Nov 4 2026\n\nThursday's front will pave the way for a cooler Friday with \ntemperatures trending down for the weekend as surface high pressure \nover the Central Plains flexes its muscle and expands south, \nreinforcing cooler continental air over our region.\n\nWhile uncertainty is still high, models have trended towards showing \na more active weather pattern for this weekend. Beginning late \nFriday, a trough is expected to amplify over the southwestern US and \ncome into phase with the subtropical jet, drawing moisture out of \nthe eastern Pacific. The ascent of this moisture as it rides above \nthe mass of cooler air over Texas is expected to result in broad \ncoverage of showers, and rain chances have increased thanks to \nbetter agreement in the model guidance. Rain chances across the \nregion range from about 40 to 60 percent. The current model \nconsensus favors the first emergence of light showers over the \nEdwards Plateau, Rio Grande Plains, and Coastal Plains Saturday \nafternoon before spreading across much of South-Central Texas \nSaturday night into Sunday. However, the timing of the end of \nthis rain event is less clear due as the speed of the amplifying \ntrough remains in contention among the ensemble guidance. Broadly,\nwe depict medium rain chances (30 to 60 percent) continuing \nthrough Sunday, diminishing Monday as the favorable ascent drops \nsouth. The cooler air, isentropic nature of the lift, and meager \nPWATs (around 1 to 1.3 inches, or near-average for mid November) \nsuggest rain rates likely won't be especially heavy. However, if \nmoisture sticks around for long enough there may be sufficient \ntime to build up beneficial rainfall totals with the soaking \nrains. About 40 percent of medium range ensembles show rain totals\nabove 1 inch for the weekend over the Rio Grande Plains, southern\nEdwards Plateau, and San Antonio area, which is a rather healthy \nproportion to be seeing at this range. \n\nThe forecast for early next week depends somewhat on how quickly the \nweekend rains come to an end, but drier weather is favored as the \ncore of the subtropical jet displaces south of the area. \nTemperatures are forecast to be near to slightly below seasonal \nnorms, with highs in the 60s to mid 70s and lows in the 40s to mid \n50s.",
